stones = [
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"Give the incidence and classification of urinary stones.",
  "a": "Affect <b>10–20%</b> of the population; males 1–2× more; <b>3rd most common urinary tract disease</b>; recurrence 50–60% within 10 years. By composition: <b>calcium 70–80%</b>, infected (struvite) 10–20%, uric acid 5–10%, cystine 1–2%, miscellaneous &lt;5%. By X-ray: radiopaque vs radiolucent (uric acid)."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"Describe the three levels of saturation and the theories of stone formation.",
  "a": "<b>Undersaturated:</b> stones dissolve; no aggregation. <b>Saturated (metastable):</b> nucleation/growth possible but inhibitors can prevent it. <b>Supersaturated:</b> inhibitors ineffective, nucleation occurs. Theories: nucleation (homogeneous or heterogeneous/epitaxy), matrix initiation (urine proteins as framework), absence of inhibitors (↓mucoprotein)."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"List the causes of calcium, uric acid and cystine stones.",
  "a": "<b>Calcium stones:</b> hypercalcemia (hyperparathyroidism, vitamin D excess, bone mets), <b>hypercalciuria (most common cause)</b>, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia, hyperuricosuria. <b>Uric acid stones:</b> hyperuricemia (gout, neoplastic disease). <b>Cystine stones:</b> autosomal recessive defect of dibasic amino acid (cysteine, lysine, arginine) reabsorption in renal tubules."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"What are the four major complications of urinary stones?",
  "a": "1. <b>Obstruction</b> (back-pressure → hydronephrosis → nephropathy), 2. <b>Infection</b> (stone + infection = <b>pyonephrosis — emergency</b>), 3. <b>Deterioration of renal function</b>, 4. <b>Chronic irritation</b> → metaplasia/malignant transformation."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"Describe the pain distribution of ureteric stones.",
  "a": "<b>Upper third:</b> flank pain with referred pain to the <b>testicle</b>. <b>Lower third:</b> flank pain, lower-quadrant pain, radiating to the <b>scrotum or vulva</b>."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"What does a plain KUB show and what is the differential of a radio-opaque shadow?",
  "a": "Diagnoses radio-opaque and soft-tissue shadows. DDx: urinary stones, gall bladder stones, phleboliths, appendicolith, GIT foreign bodies, nephrocalcinosis, calcified renal cyst/tumor, calcified mesenteric nodes, chip fracture of transverse process/rib."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"What are the indications and contraindications of ESWL?",
  "a": "Principle: acoustic shock waves focused on the stone (X-ray/US) fragment it into particles. Indication: stones <b>≤2 cm</b>. Contraindications: urological (associated pathology needing intervention, acute UTI, distal obstruction, larger stones, renal insufficiency, single renal unit unless stented) and non-urological (pregnancy, bleeding disorders…)."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"What are the indications for PCNL?",
  "a": "Stones <b>&gt;2 cm</b>; staghorn stones; ESWL failure; associated pathology manipulable percutaneously (e.g., PUJO). Procedure: puncture calyx → guide wire → dilate tract (Amplatz sheath) → nephroscope → stone extraction or lithotripsy → nephrostomy 1–2 days."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"How are ureteric stones managed by site?",
  "a": "<b>Upper third:</b> 1) ESWL, 2) ureteroscopy + laser, 3) open surgery. <b>Middle third:</b> 1) ureteroscopy + laser, 2) ESWL, 3) open. <b>Lower third:</b> 1) ureteroscopy, 2) ESWL, 3) open."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"When is open surgery (cystolithotomy) indicated for bladder stones?",
  "a": "Large stones not manipulable endoscopically; multiple or very hard stones; diverticular stones; pediatric bladder calculi (without laser); associated pathology needing open surgery. Otherwise: endoscopic litholapaxy or lithotripsy."},
 {"cat":"Stones","q":"What is the metabolic work-up and prevention of stone recurrence?",
  "a": "Serum Ca/P (exclude hyperparathyroidism), uric acid (gout); 24-h urine calcium (300 mg), uric acid (800 mg), oxalate (40 mg), citrate (300–900 mg) for a 60-kg adult. General: high fluid intake, restrict dietary salt/protein, avoid obesity. Calcium oxalate: low-oxalate diet, HCTZ 50 mg bid, citrates 5 g bid. Uric acid: avoid purines, keep urine alkaline (NaHCO₃), rule out myeloproliferative disease."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"What is significant bacteriuria and the gold standard of UTI diagnosis?",
  "a": "Gold standard: <b>detection of the pathogen (midstream urine culture) in the presence of clinical symptoms</b>. Thresholds: uncomplicated cystitis ≥10³ cfu/mL; pyelonephritis ≥10⁴ cfu/mL; classic significant bacteriuria ≥10⁵ cfu/mL (also in pyelonephritis of pregnancy, Kass); complicated UTI (men) ≥10⁴; any count on suprapubic puncture."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"What is the pathogenesis of UTI and catheter-related risk?",
  "a": "<b>E. coli causes 85% of ambulatory and 50% of nosocomial UTIs</b>; then Proteus, Klebsiella, Enterococcus. Route: <b>retrograde ascent from fecal flora</b> via urethra to bladder/kidney. Single catheter insertion → UTI in 1–2%; indwelling catheters with open drainage → bacteriuria in nearly 100% within 3–4 days. Hematogenous spread only for a few microbes (S. aureus, Candida)."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"Define uncomplicated vs complicated UTI.",
  "a": "<b>Uncomplicated:</b> acute cystitis/pyelonephritis in otherwise healthy individuals (E. coli 70–95%, S. saprophyticus 5–10%). <b>Complicated:</b> structural/functional abnormalities, indwelling catheters/stents, obstructive uropathy, renal insufficiency, transplant, diabetes, immunodeficiency, VUR, urinary diversions (ileal loop)."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"How is acute uncomplicated cystitis treated?",
  "a": "<b>Cotrimoxazole 160/800 mg bid × 3 days</b> (classic first-line) or trimethoprim 200 mg × 5 days; alternative ciprofloxacin 250 mg bid when resistance/intolerance. Diagnosis: ≥10³ cfu/mL; culture if suspected pyelonephritis or symptoms don't resolve/recur within 2–4 weeks."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"How is acute uncomplicated pyelonephritis treated?",
  "a": "Diagnosis ≥10⁴ cfu/mL; US to exclude obstruction/stones; CT or DMSA if persistent. Mild: <b>oral fluoroquinolone × 7 days</b>. Severe: admission, <b>parenteral fluoroquinolone or 3rd-generation cephalosporin</b>, then oral continuation 1–2 weeks."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"How are UTIs managed in pregnancy?",
  "a": "Asymptomatic bacteriuria in 4–7% of pregnant women; <b>20–40% progress to pyelonephritis</b> — treat asymptomatic bacteriuria. Prophylaxis: low-dose cephalexin (125–250 mg) or nitrofurantoin (50 mg) nightly. Acute pyelonephritis: 2nd/3rd-gen cephalosporins or aminoglycoside. <b>Quinolones, tetracyclines and TMP are contraindicated in the 1st trimester.</b>"},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"What are the three groups of recurrent UTI?",
  "a": "<b>1. Unresolved infection</b> — subtherapeutic antibiotic, non-compliance, malabsorption, resistant pathogens. <b>2. Bacterial persistence</b> — a nidus in the urinary tract (may need surgical correction). <b>3. Reinfection</b> — new infection from periurethral/perineal/rectal flora. In post-menopausal women: <b>intravaginal oestriol</b> significantly reduces recurrence."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"Describe urosepsis and its treatment.",
  "a": "SIRS (fever/hypothermia, leucocytosis/leucopenia, tachycardia, tachypnoea) is the first event → sepsis/septic shock → multi-organ failure. Prognosis better than sepsis from other sites but mortality up to <b>20–40%</b>. Treatment: resuscitation + source control (relieve obstruction, drainage) + antibiotics."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"How are gonorrhoea and chlamydial urethritis treated?",
  "a": "<b>Gonorrhoea:</b> ceftriaxone 125 mg IM single dose (or cefixime 400 mg, ciprofloxacin 500 mg, ofloxacin 400 mg, levofloxacin 250 mg orally). <b>Chlamydia:</b> azithromycin 1 g single dose or doxycycline 100 mg bid × 7 days. <b>Always dual therapy for both</b> — co-infection is common."},
 {"cat":"UTI","q":"Give the NIDDK/NIH classification of prostatitis and treatment.",
  "a": "<b>I:</b> acute bacterial prostatitis (admission + parenteral antibiotics, then oral 2–4 weeks). <b>II:</b> chronic bacterial prostatitis. <b>III:</b> chronic abacterial prostatitis/CPPS (A: inflammatory — white cells; B: non-inflammatory). <b>IV:</b> asymptomatic inflammatory prostatitis. Chronic: <b>ciprofloxacin/levofloxacin 4–6 weeks</b> (best pharmacokinetics)."},
]

